bootable install diskettes
==========================

	make bootdisks

at the top of the tree builds three, here:

	d8ss.img	8 inch, single sided	77 cyl, 1 head,  15 x 512	1125 blocks
	d8ds.img	8 inch, double sided	77 cyl, 2 heads, 15 x 512	2250 blocks
	d5ds.img	5 1/4, double sided	40 cyl, 2 heads, 10 x 512	 760 blocks

They are build output, regenerated from the tree the way a .vol is, and
.gitignore drops them; the script that makes them is the only real file
in this directory.  Nothing here is the distributed standalone: see
REGEN.md in ../hdinstall for what patching that costs and for what is left
open there.

The five inch single sided medium is deliberately absent.  194 K is not
a running system, and a diskette that can only be a data carrier is not
worth a media row.

what one is
-----------

A whole small micronix, on one diskette, whose root is the diskette.
The loader is in the sectors in front of the filesystem and the
filesystem behind it, and one struct dlabel at byte 256 of block 0
describes both - so the diskette says what it is rather than the name it
is reached by.  A reader with the file in its hands and no device number
at all can mount it.  See docs/DISKLABEL.md for the label,
and src/micronix/stand/boot/README for the loader.

To boot one:

	cd ../../src/hwsim/d1
	./d1 -B djdma -5 <the d5ds image>

The bare filename of an eight inch image goes on the eight inch port;
the five inch one needs -5.  Both are unskewed raw images - the DJ's
alternate sectoring bit is clear - which is what the loader and the host
tools both read.  -B djdma is the boot device; the monitor rom and the
level 1 rom the chain starts from are the simulator's own defaults, and
-b and -M name others.

what is on it, and why so little
--------------------------------

The install diskette is not a tool bag.  hdcdmainit mkfs's a hard disk,
mounts it on /b and copies the running system across with cptree, so
what has to fit is the smallest micronix that can do that, and the
medium it has to fit in is 760 blocks:

	/micronix	the kernel, 128 K (131072 bytes), 257 blocks
			with its indirect block
	/bootmw.bin	the controller's 4k level 2 boot the install writes
			into the new drive's boot area - mkfs.h's DEFBOOT,
			read from the diskette that is running the install.
			It is bootimg for an hdcdma target, and the target's
			own image for the others
	/hdcdmainit	the install script, 382 bytes
	/.sh		the shell's path, 19 bytes
	/bin		the thirteen commands an install needs:
			sh rm mkfs mount umount chmod cptree mknod
			setdev ln sync echo format halt
	/dev		every node in ../../src/micronix/dev/devlist,
			including kmem; the directory is three blocks
	/etc		the config and init, 13 files, 57 blocks
	/b /f /tmp	the mount points hdcdmainit and finstall use, and a
			scratch directory

That is 532 of the 760 blocks on the five inch, leaving 228 free.  The
eight inch carries the same kernel and the same install set; it is not
a bigger toolkit, it is the same article on a medium with more room.

format low-level formats a hard disk on any of the controllers - the
HD-DMA, the HDCA, the 5380 or the IDE card - or a DJ-DMA diskette,
which is done to the drive before the diskette installs onto it.  It is
one binary where there used to be four, and the install diskette
carries it: a drive has to be laid down before mkfs can read its
label, and the operator runs format with the model or geometry the
drive is.  cat and the operator commands are not needed by an install.
chg and fdj are what makes a *booted* diskette able to make diskettes,
with "chg djdma:1 blank:dist-1.img; fdj format djdma:1; mount it; copy
data; umount" and no host tool at all.  See sys/MEDIACHG.md.  They are
on the maintenance diskette, with the repair tools.

fsck is not on the install diskette.  An install makes a new
filesystem; checking the one it just made is not a step that can repair
anything, and the block it costs is better spent on the formatter.
label is not there either: format writes the label and its prototype
table.  The tools for repairing an existing hard disk are on the
maintenance diskette:

	./create_bootdisk -m d8ds /tmp/maintenance.img hdcdma

which is the same boot and root with fsck, label, format and the file
tools, and without the install script.  The five inch maintenance set
leaves out login and the diskette loop; it is still fsck, label,
format, mount and the file tools, and it fits.

So the install knapsack is exactly the install tooling, with room to
spare on both media.  The maintenance diskette is the other article:
fsck, label, format, the file tools and the diskette loop.

install, from the diskette
--------------------------

Boot it, wait for the welcome and then for the shell's "#".  The drive
must carry a label before mkfs can read its geometry; if it does not,
format lays down the geometry and the prototype slice table.  For an
HD-DMA drive whose model is m40:

	format -m m40 /dev/hd0c

The IDE and SCSI cards are given the geometry with -c, -h and -s, and
the HDCA has its own models; a drive that already has a label keeps the
geometry in it and can skip this.

Then type

	source hdcdmainit

which mkfs's /dev/hd0a, mounts it on /b, copies the system across with
cptree, points the copy's kernel at the drive with setdev, relinks
/dev/root, and unmounts.  It prints "Disk initialization complete."
when it is done, and cptree is copying a filesystem through a simulated
Z80, so give it time.

The drive's boot area is below slice a and a guest mkfs leaves it to the
host, so write it first - for an hdcdma drive:

	mnix hardboot <volume> src/micronix/stand/boot/bootimg

Then boot the drive:

	./d1 -c 0x0c hdcdma0:<volume>

which is the HD-DMA path with the monitor skipped - the configuration
switch 0x0c everything used to be written down as.  See ../hdinstall
for the volume to point it at and for the rest of that run.

"source hdcdmainit" rather than "hdcdmainit", because the console
shell's path is /bin and /usr/bin and the script is in the root
directory.  source is a builtin and reads the file where you name it.

The diskette is one article for one controller.  create_bootdisk also
takes ide, scsi and hdca, and each carries that controller's init
script and its own 4k level 2 boot under /bootmw.bin:

	./create_bootdisk d8ds /tmp/d8ds-ide.img ide

The boot area is below slice a, so the installed drive boots only after
the boot image is written there.  A guest mkfs leaves that to the host,
which is what "mnix hardboot" does; the matrix harness does it after
the install boot, before it boots the drive.

what is not here
----------------

	/.login		rootfiles/.login is "cd /usr/src; make" - the
			volume's build login, which on a diskette with no
			sources would only fail
	era		a Whitesmiths command this tree has never built; the
			install uses rm -f where the original used it
	a compiler, sources, man pages, and the rest of /bin - a system
	with a hard disk installs those from the media that carry them

the label, and the format byte that used to be read
---------------------------------------------------

A hard sectored medium's geometry cannot be worked out from the drive;
the controller does not report it.  What says it now is the disk label:
mkbootimg writes one into the second half of block 0 - the block the
loader is in - and the kernel's dj.c reads it with dllabel() before it
reads anything else (sys/dj.c's djopen).  d5ds carries 40 cylinders,
two heads, ten sectors of 512 bytes, first sector 0.

The one thing the label cannot say is where it is.  Whether the boot
track numbers its sectors from 0 or 1 decides which sector block 0 is,
and that is the drive's to report rather than the medium's - the
characteristics byte's HARD bit - so djopen sets firstsec from the drive
and then reads the label with it.  The loader has no drive to ask, so it
takes the number from the label instead, which is why mkbootimg writes
it there.

A diskette with no label does not come up with a wrong geometry.  The
boot stops at the loader - "No disk label in the block this boot came
from" - and the kernel's own open leaves the drive mapping block 0 and
nothing else, so the first read past it is what fails rather than the
one at block 330 where the overlay modules are.

The eight inch media carry the same label and differ only in its
numbers: soft sectored, first sector 1, fifteen blocks to a track.
src/micronix/stand/boot/GNUmakefile's FLOPPIES table is what says so per
medium, and mkbootimg is what writes it.

